format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-meets-matter

lesson becquerel-gray-sievert
title: Becquerel, gray and sievert
minutes: 6
xp: 50
teaches: u3-three-units
teaches: u3-unit-not-dose
relies_on: u3-detectors

description:
    Three units answer three different questions about radiation. You will learn what the becquerel, the gray and the sievert each measure, and why a big number in one tells you nothing about the others.

cite ref-openstax-chem-21-6

supports:
    The becquerel is one disintegration per second; the gray is one joule of energy absorbed per kilogram of tissue; the sievert takes into account both the energy and the biological effects of the type of radiation, and measures tissue damage.

cite ref-who-ionizing-radiation

supports:
    The becquerel measures activity, the gray measures absorbed dose and the sievert measures the biological effect of radiation.

card c01
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Three questions, three units
figure: three-questions
figure_at: 1

body:
    Picture a cloud, a bucket and a garden. The cloud drops rain. The bucket catches some. The garden may or may not be harmed by it. Radiation has three questions like that, and each one has its own unit.

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Becquerel: how active the source is
figure: becquerel-figure
figure_at: 1

body:
    The [[becquerel|becquerel]] tells you how active a source is. One becquerel is one decay per second. So a source of 20 becquerels has 20 nuclei decaying every second.

card c03
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l5-fill-bq
connected: true

card c04
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: A big number is about the source

body:
    A becquerel counts what the source is doing, like the cloud counting its drops. It does not say how much radiation reached you, or what it did. Those have their own units.

card c05
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l5-tom-bq-dose
connected: true

card c06
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Gray: energy absorbed
figure: gray-figure
figure_at: 1

body:
    The [[gray|gray]] measures energy absorbed, like the water in the bucket. One gray is one joule of energy absorbed by each kilogram. It does not say what kind of radiation the energy came from.

card c07
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l5-choice-gray
connected: true

card c08
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Sievert: potential for harm
figure: sievert-figure
figure_at: 1

body:
    The [[sievert|sievert]] measures the potential for harm, like what the rain does to the garden. It counts the energy absorbed, and also the type of radiation and how sensitive the tissue is.

